      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical substance composed of two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom. Its chemical formula is **$H_2O$**.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what makes water unique and important:\n\n### 1. Chemical Structure\n*   **Composition:** It is made of two hydrogen atoms bonded to a single oxygen atom.\n*   **Polarity:** The oxygen atom is more \"electronegative\" (it pulls electrons toward itself) than the hydrogen atoms. This creates a \"bent\" molecular shape where one side of the molecule has a slight positive charge and the other has a slight negative charge. This polarity makes water an excellent **solvent**, meaning it can dissolve more substances than any other liquid.\n\n### 2. Physical States\nWater is the only common substance on Earth that naturally exists as a solid, liquid, and gas at temperatures commonly found on the planet:\n*   **Solid (Ice):** When frozen, water molecules form a rigid, crystalline structure.\n*   **Liquid:** This is the state we use for drinking and washing.\n*   **Gas (Steam/Vapor):** When heated, water turns into a gas that rises into the atmosphere.\n\n### 3. Unique Properties\nWater has several properties that make life possible:\n*   **High Surface Tension:** Water molecules stick together strongly at the surface, allowing insects to walk on water and for water to form droplets.\n*   **High Specific Heat:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before getting hot. This helps regulate the Earth's temperature and keeps the human body from overheating.\n*   **Density Anomaly:** Unlike most substances, water is **less dense** as a solid (ice) than as a liquid. This is why ice floats on water, insulating lakes and oceans in winter to keep aquatic life alive.\n\n### 4. Abundance and Importance\nWater covers about 71% of the Earth's surface. It is the \"universal solvent\" of biology, acting as the medium for chemical reactions in cells, transporting nutrients, and removing waste. Without water, life as we know it could not exist."
